  "teamId": 116,
  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 Detroit Tigers entered the season with playoff aspirations, but a bullpen anchored by a struggling Kenley Jansen quickly revealed itself as the team's fatal flaw. Opening Day set an ominous tone when Jansen surrendered a three-run homer, and the pattern would repeat itself with devastating regularity throughout the year. Despite a talented offensive core led by Riley Greene, Spencer Torkelson, and Kerry Carpenter, the Tigers could never fully outrun the damage done in late innings.\n\nApril featured a whiplash quality that defined the Tigers' entire season. Jansen's meltdowns cost the team multiple games, yet Greene's clutch double, Colt Keith's timely single, Carpenter's go-ahead blast, and Torkelson's emerging power gave Detroit fans genuine reasons for hope. The team was competitive but fragile, winning games in the afternoon that their bullpen would give back by nightfall.\n\nThe summer months told a story of gradual erosion. From late May through July, opposing hitters feasted on a patchwork pitching staff that cycled through Drew Anderson, Will Vest, and Jacob Waguespack with little success. Bright spots like Kevin McGonigle's clutch single, Hao-Yu Lee's reviewed double, and James Outman's three-run homer kept the Tigers in the wild card hunt longer than their record might suggest, but each promising moment was answered by another bullpen disaster.\n\nBy September, the math had become insurmountable. Jansen continued to be torched into August and early September, while Drew Sommers and Kyle Finnegan added their own costly contributions to a bullpen ERA that undermined a surprisingly solid 3.67 team ERA overall. Eduardo Valencia's late walk-off homer on September 26th was a bittersweet reminder of what might have been — a team with enough offense and starting pitching to compete, ultimately sunk by an inability to close out games in a 76-86 campaign that fell well short of October baseball.",
